01:17:52.090 --> 01:17:52.830
Come on, let's get moving.

01:17:52.870 --> 01:17:55.110
Oh, wait a minute, Mr. Reynolds. I wouldn't be any use to you.

01:17:55.110 --> 01:17:57.550
You underestimate yourself, Mr. Waterbury.

01:17:57.790 --> 01:18:00.000
We have a lot of use for the only living newborns.

01:18:00.030 --> 01:18:01.480
a nuclear chain reaction?

01:18:01.480 --> 01:18:03.150
Come on, let's get going.

01:18:03.150 --> 01:18:05.100
Now, wait a minute. Aren't you forgetting something, Reynolds?

01:18:05.100 --> 01:18:07.040
You wouldn't dare fire that gun at me.

01:18:07.040 --> 01:18:07.930
Don't tempt me.

01:18:07.930 --> 01:18:10.930
You fire that gun at me and it might cause a chain reaction

01:18:10.930 --> 01:18:13.410
and I'd explode all over you.

01:18:13.410 --> 01:18:16.310
Yes, do you know that they examined me just this afternoon

01:18:16.310 --> 01:18:17.310
for radioactivity?

01:18:17.310 --> 01:18:18.310
And you know what it was?

01:18:18.310 --> 01:18:21.810
It was pi r squared over four minus one.

01:18:21.810 --> 01:18:24.310
And if you're any kind of a scientist, Mr. Reynolds,

01:18:24.310 --> 01:18:26.810
you'll know that that's darn near the exploding point.

01:18:26.810 --> 01:18:30.000
No, no, don't get excited, Tumpkin.

01:18:30.000 --> 01:18:31.760
Remember, if you go off, we all go.

01:18:31.760 --> 01:18:33.560
I'm feeling a little on edge, Stan.

01:18:33.560 --> 01:18:36.040
Yes, if anything happens, the least little thing sets me off.

01:18:36.040 --> 01:18:37.320
This whole room's liable to go up and smoke.

01:18:37.320 --> 01:18:39.220
I just hope I don't even hiccup.

01:18:39.220 --> 01:18:41.650
You don't expect me to fall for that comic book stuff.

01:18:41.650 --> 01:18:42.830
Get going.

01:18:42.830 --> 01:18:45.350
If we could only stall them until 5 o'clock, Stan.

01:18:45.350 --> 01:18:46.890
What happened at 5 o'clock?

01:18:46.890 --> 01:18:49.160
What are you two mumbling about?

01:18:49.160 --> 01:18:51.370
We were just talking about my hiccups.

01:18:51.370 --> 01:18:54.280
My hiccups are terrible, but if I sneeze, it's disastrous.

01:18:54.280 --> 01:18:57.040
One sneeze, and I could blow up a whole city block.

01:18:57.040 --> 01:18:59.600
All right, go ahead and sneeze.

01:19:00.640 --> 01:19:07.620
Well, uh, it takes me a few seconds to work it up here a little, like...

01:19:07.620 --> 01:19:10.780
You know what I think, Waterbury? I think you're a phony.

01:19:10.780 --> 01:19:13.280
Just scare propaganda put out by the government.

01:19:13.280 --> 01:19:14.680
That's right, he is a phony.

01:19:14.680 --> 01:19:17.350
And you don't want to have anything to do with a phony, do you, Mr. Reynolds?

01:19:17.350 --> 01:19:18.630
So let's get out of here, you phony.

01:19:18.630 --> 01:19:21.630
Wait a minute, I feel a hiccup coming on.

01:19:21.630 --> 01:19:27.820
Excuse me, must have been something I ate.

01:19:27.820 --> 01:19:29.800
You get the idea, Mr. Reynolds?

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:40.310
neutrons are warming up. The chain is beginning to form. I'm gonna sneeze! Run for the hills!

01:20:02.750 --> 01:20:03.730
Thank you, Dr. Rodell.

01:20:12.090 --> 01:20:13.930
Now, Mr. Reynolds, do you still think I'm a phony?

01:20:14.010 --> 01:20:15.590
It's a good thing I stifled that sneeze.

01:20:15.970 --> 01:20:17.090
You're not human.

01:20:18.810 --> 01:20:20.050
Are we still alive?

01:20:22.140 --> 01:20:23.320
You better hand over that gun.

01:20:23.500 --> 01:20:25.220
Yeah, hand it over and be quick.

01:20:25.440 --> 01:20:26.710
What do you think, you're playing with kids?

01:20:26.810 --> 01:20:28.270
You said you were going to take us some other place.

01:20:28.330 --> 01:20:28.790
Where was it?

01:20:28.870 --> 01:20:30.000
I can't tell you.

01:20:30.000 --> 01:20:31.980
You better tell me, if you think that sneeze was anything,

01:20:31.980 --> 01:20:34.120
wait till you see me ram my head against the wall.

01:20:34.120 --> 01:20:36.080
- No, wait, I'll tell you, I'll tell you.

01:20:36.080 --> 01:20:38.080
I was taking you to the room right over this.

01:20:38.080 --> 01:20:39.080
- Yeah, who's in the room?

01:20:39.080 --> 01:20:41.480
- My superior, comrade, Mr. Mosley.

01:20:41.480 --> 01:20:42.220
- Well, get him on the phone

01:20:42.220 --> 01:20:44.620
and tell him to come down here right away.

01:20:44.620 --> 01:20:49.860
Go on.

01:20:49.860 --> 01:20:53.500
- Operator, operator, get me room 412.

01:20:53.500 --> 01:20:55.560
- Hey, Tempton, don't go ramming your head against the wall.

01:20:55.560 --> 01:20:57.030
Remember, it's our lives too.

01:20:57.030 --> 01:20:59.090
- Listen, Stan, it was just a bluff.

01:20:59.090 --> 01:21:00.000
Dr. Rodell said that I'm

01:21:00.000 --> 01:21:02.200
I'm cured. I've lost all of my radioactivity.

01:21:02.200 --> 01:21:03.540
What a relief.

01:21:03.540 --> 01:21:05.710
Why have your radioactivity gone?

01:21:05.710 --> 01:21:07.110
Shh, be quiet. You want to get us killed?

01:21:07.110 --> 01:21:08.900
Hello, Mosley.

01:21:08.900 --> 01:21:11.010
You've got to come down here right away.

01:21:11.010 --> 01:21:12.560
It's important.

01:21:12.560 --> 01:21:14.430
I'll tell you when you get here.

01:21:14.430 --> 01:21:16.030
Hurry.

01:21:16.030 --> 01:21:18.510
Now all we have to do is to wait for the head guy to get here,

01:21:18.510 --> 01:21:21.010
then tie you fellas up and hand you over to the FBI.

01:21:21.010 --> 01:21:22.000
Yeah.

01:21:22.000 --> 01:21:23.910
Then we'll be heroes again.

01:21:23.910 --> 01:21:26.980
We can sell our life story to the movies.

01:21:26.980 --> 01:21:27.990
Let's see.

01:21:27.990 --> 01:21:29.960
Clark Gable should play me.

01:21:30.220 --> 01:21:35.660
Of course, we'll find somebody to play you.

01:21:35.660 --> 01:21:38.220
No, we haven't had a day off in so long, I don't know how to act.

01:21:38.220 --> 01:21:41.640
And what makes it so enjoyable is that we've seen the last of Waterbury.

01:21:41.640 --> 01:21:45.520
Please, please, don't mention that name.

01:21:45.520 --> 01:21:47.670
You know, Jumpkin, on second thought,

01:21:47.670 --> 01:21:50.330
I don't think we can find anybody to play you.

01:21:50.330 --> 01:21:52.090
We may have to write you out of the picture entirely.

01:21:52.090 --> 01:21:54.010
I don't care if anybody ever plays my life story.

01:21:54.010 --> 01:21:56.850
After this is all over, Audrey and me, we're going to be married.

01:21:56.850 --> 01:21:59.280
Can you imagine Audrey and me married?

01:22:01.180 --> 01:22:02.180
Hey, was that Mosley?

01:22:02.180 --> 01:22:03.180
I can't tell you.

01:22:03.180 --> 01:22:04.180
I'll sneeze again.

01:22:04.180 --> 01:22:05.180
That was Mosley.

01:22:05.180 --> 01:22:06.180
Yeah.

01:22:06.180 --> 01:22:08.700
Listen.

01:22:08.700 --> 01:22:10.070
Help anybody!

01:22:10.070 --> 01:22:11.070
Sounds like Waterbury.

01:22:11.070 --> 01:22:12.070
No, it couldn't be.

01:22:12.070 --> 01:22:16.040
Watch out for a man in a black hat that might be coming out of the hotel any minute!

01:22:24.090 --> 01:22:26.770
Be right down!

01:22:26.770 --> 01:22:29.730
Oh, no you don't.

01:23:01.500 --> 01:23:08.080
listen to this in a spectacular feat of heroism blicks waterberry the former atomic kid dived

01:23:08.080 --> 01:23:12.800
out of a third story hotel window today to apprehend the leading agent of an international

01:23:12.800 --> 01:23:19.590
espionage ring the capture took place under the startled eyes of two fbi men disguised as tennis

01:23:19.590 --> 01:23:23.990
players stan should have gotten all the credit if it hadn't been for his sock on the jaw none of

01:23:23.990 --> 01:23:30.000
this could have happened wait a minute there's more waterberry's friend stan cooper who assisted in rounding up the

01:23:30.000 --> 01:23:34.240
the spy ring, left late today for Hollywood, where he's going to consult with studio officials

01:23:34.240 --> 01:23:36.700
about a picture based on his life.

01:23:36.700 --> 01:23:41.080
He said he thinks Clark Gable will play the leading role.

01:23:41.080 --> 01:23:43.970
I wonder if Gable's good enough.

01:23:43.970 --> 01:23:48.670
Hero Waterbury and his former nurse, Audrey Nelson, were married here today before a justice

01:23:48.670 --> 01:23:51.650
of the peace and left for an extended honeymoon.

01:23:51.650 --> 01:23:53.860
Gee, I wish they hadn't have printed that.

01:23:53.860 --> 01:23:56.500
I didn't want anybody to know where we were going, that's why I'm taking all these back

01:23:56.500 --> 01:23:57.670
roads.

01:23:57.670 --> 01:23:59.960
Are you sure you know where you're going, Blix?

01:24:00.350 --> 01:24:02.730
Sure, we ought to be hitting San Francisco any minute now.

01:24:03.890 --> 01:24:06.490
We'd better stop at the next house we see and ask for directions.

01:24:07.680 --> 01:24:09.020
All right, Mrs. Waterbury.

01:24:11.450 --> 01:24:14.110
Hey, oh, there's one up there.

01:24:20.290 --> 01:24:25.150
Come on in with me, honey.

01:24:26.210 --> 01:24:30.000
We'll ask the folks inside for the directions and maybe they'll give us...

01:24:30.000 --> 01:24:33.520
- Give us a cool drink. - All right, Blakes.

01:24:46.060 --> 01:24:47.830
On your glasses.

01:24:47.830 --> 01:24:51.030
The plane dropping the H-bomb is approaching the target now.

01:24:56.590 --> 01:24:58.260
No!
